---
/**
 * This page used to run its own inbox/domain/cost calculator. Warm Inboxes
 * already has that tool at /calculator (and a ramp-aware version at
 * /inbox-planner), so prewarmed.org does not rebuild it and compete with it.
 * What lives here instead is the arithmetic, stated so a reader can do it on
 * paper, plus the one decision the calculators do not make for you: packs or
 * a tenant. The worked example is computed from pricing.json so it cannot
 * drift away from the prices published elsewhere on the site.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { wi, toolUrl } from '../../data/site.js';
import pricing from '../../data/pricing.json';
import tools from '../../data/tools.json';
import { rate } from '../../data/volume.js';

const planner = tools.tools.find((t) => t.id === 'inbox-planner');
const pack = pricing.products['google-prewarmed'];
const tenant = pricing.products['azure-tenant'];

// One worked example, carried through every step below.
const ex = { target: 1500, perInbox: rate('google'), perDomain: 3, headroom: 0.2 };
ex.base = Math.ceil(ex.target / ex.perInbox);
ex.inboxes = Math.ceil(ex.base * (1 + ex.headroom));
ex.domains = Math.ceil(ex.inboxes / ex.perDomain);
ex.packs = Math.ceil(ex.inboxes / pack.packSize);
ex.packCost = ex.inboxes * pack.perUnit.quarterly;
// A tenant mailbox runs far quieter than a pack inbox, so it needs its own count.
ex.tenantPace = rate('azure-100');
ex.tenantMailboxes = Math.ceil(Math.ceil(ex.target / ex.tenantPace) * (1 + ex.headroom));
ex.tenants = Math.ceil(ex.tenantMailboxes / tenant.inboxesPerTenant);
ex.tenantCost = ex.tenants * tenant.perTenant.quarterly;
const money = (n) => `$${n.toLocaleString('en-US')}`;

const calcUrl = wi('/calculator', { page: 'stack-calculator', placement: 'handoff' });

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [{
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      ['How many inboxes do I need?', `Divide your daily send target by the safe pace for the platform: ${rate('google')} a day on Google Workspace, ${rate('microsoft')} on Microsoft 365, ${ex.tenantPace} per mailbox on a full Azure tenant. Then add about 20 per cent spare. At ${ex.target} a day on Google that is ${ex.inboxes} inboxes.`],
      ['How many domains do I need?', `Three inboxes per domain. At ${ex.inboxes} inboxes that is ${ex.domains} domains.`],
      ['Packs or an Azure tenant?', `Packs keep a failure contained to three inboxes. A tenant is far cheaper per inbox but all ${tenant.inboxesPerTenant} share one reputation. Under about 1,000 sends a day, buy packs.`],
    ].map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })),
  }],
};
---
<Base title="How Many Inboxes and Domains Do You Need? The Arithmetic"
      description="The four-step calculation behind cold email infrastructure sizing, with a worked example, plus the one decision a calculator cannot make for you: packs or a tenant."
      schema={schema}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <p class="crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> <span class="muted">›</span> <a href="/tools/">Free tools</a> <span class="muted">›</span> <span class="muted">Sizing</span></p>
      <p class="eyebrow">Planning</p>
      <h1>How many inboxes and domains do you actually need?</h1>
      <p class="lede">It is four lines of arithmetic. Below is the method, a worked example, and the one decision the number on its own will not make for you.</p>

      <div class="handoff">
        <p><strong>Want it worked out for you?</strong> Warm Inboxes already runs this as a calculator. Put your target in and it returns the inboxes, domains and cost. We are not going to build a second one.</p>
        <p class="handoff-links">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={calcUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the calculator at Warm Inboxes ↗</a>
          {planner && <a href={toolUrl(planner, { page: 'stack-calculator', placement: 'handoff' })} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{planner.name} ↗</a>}
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The four steps</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Inboxes: divide your daily target by the pace</h3>
          <p>{rate('google')} cold emails per inbox per day on Google Workspace, {rate('microsoft')} on Microsoft 365, {ex.tenantPace} per mailbox on a full Azure tenant. Those are what we run and what we recommend, and they are deliberately low. The published provider limits are the point at which Google or Microsoft stop you, not advice on what is safe.</p>
          <p class="ex mono">{ex.target.toLocaleString('en-US')} ÷ {ex.perInbox} = {ex.base} inboxes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Add about 20 per cent for replacements</h3>
          <p>Inboxes go down. If you size to exactly your target, the first domain you lose puts you under it, and you are then buying replacements in a hurry, which is when people skip the checks. Hold some back.</p>
          <p class="ex mono">{ex.base} + 20% = {ex.inboxes} inboxes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Domains: three inboxes on each</h3>
          <p>Three is the number that keeps a failure small. When a domain stops landing, everything on it goes with it, so the question is how much you lose at once. More inboxes per domain is cheaper and concentrates the damage.</p>
          <p class="ex mono">{ex.inboxes} ÷ {ex.perDomain} = {ex.domains} domains</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Cost: price both shapes, not one</h3>
          <p>The same volume costs wildly different amounts depending on whether you buy packs or tenants, and the cheaper one is not automatically the right one. That is the next section.</p>
          <p class="ex mono">{ex.packs} packs of {pack.packSize}, or {ex.tenants} {ex.tenants === 1 ? 'tenant' : 'tenants'}</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>The decision a calculator will not make for you</h2>
      <p>At {ex.target.toLocaleString('en-US')} sends a day you need {ex.inboxes} Google inboxes in packs, or {ex.tenantMailboxes} mailboxes across Azure tenants, because a tenant mailbox sends {ex.tenantPace} a day rather than {ex.perInbox}. The two shapes are not the same product at different prices.</p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Packs</th><th>Azure {ex.tenants === 1 ? 'tenant' : 'tenants'}</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>What you buy</td><td>{ex.packs} packs of {pack.packSize}, {ex.perInbox} a day each</td><td>{ex.tenants} {ex.tenants === 1 ? 'tenant' : 'tenants'} of {tenant.inboxesPerTenant}, {ex.tenantPace} a day each</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Roughly per month</td><td>{money(ex.packCost)}</td><td class="yes">{money(ex.tenantCost)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Domains involved</td><td class="yes">{ex.domains}</td><td>Far fewer</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What one failure costs you</td><td class="yes">{pack.packSize} inboxes</td><td class="no">Up to {tenant.inboxesPerTenant} at once</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Who it suits</td><td>Most teams</td><td>Operators who already run a tenant carefully</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>A tenant is the cheaper line item by a long way, and it asks something in return: all {tenant.inboxesPerTenant} mailboxes share one reputation, so a single unverified list can damage every one of them at the same time. Under about 1,000 sends a day the saving is not worth that, and you should buy packs. Above it, buy a tenant and run it properly, meaning verified lists only, mailboxes started in batches, bounce rate checked daily for the first fortnight.</p>
      <p>The full risk model is on the <a href="/prewarmed-azure-inboxes/">Azure tenant page</a>, and the two are compared directly in <a href="/compare/azure-tenant-vs-microsoft-365/">Azure tenant vs Microsoft 365 packs</a>.</p>

      <h2>Then check what waiting costs you</h2>
      <p>Sizing tells you what to buy. It does not tell you whether to buy it prewarmed or fresh, and that decision usually turns on the value of the weeks rather than the price of the mailbox. The <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a> prices both against your start date.</p>

      <p class="cta-row"><a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href={calcUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the numbers at Warm Inboxes ↗</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .narrow { max-width: 780px; }
  .crumb { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--text-2); margin: 0 0 26px; }
  h1 { margin: 12px 0 18px; }
  h2 { margin: 44px 0 16px; }
  p { color: var(--text-2); }
  .handoff { margin: 30px 0 8px; background: var(--bg-elev); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--r-card); padding: 22px 24px; }
  .handoff p { margin: 0; font-size: 16px; }
  .handoff strong { color: var(--text); }
  .handoff-links { margin-top: 18px !important; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 20px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .handoff-links a:not(.btn) { font-size: 14.5px; font-weight: 500; }
  .steps { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; counter-reset: s; }
  .steps li { counter-increment: s; position: relative; padding: 0 0 26px 46px; }
  .steps li::before { content: counter(s); position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; width: 30px; height: 30px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent); font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; display: grid; place-items: center; }
  .steps h3 { font-size: 17.5px; margin: 4px 0 10px; }
  .steps p { font-size: 15.5px; margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .ex { background: var(--bg-elev); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; padding: 10px 14px; color: var(--text) !important; font-size: 15px; display: inline-block; }
  .cta-row { margin-top: 40px; }
</style>
